course: dictionary
lesson: 5216
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Jazz can be a noun, the name of a kind of music, or a verb, as in jazz up, which means to make something brighter or more exciting. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «Jazz players make up solos on the spot after years of practice.» Players and solos are music words, so jazz must mean a kind of music here. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: Phrases also have their own meanings. «And all that jazz» does not mean anything about music. It means and things like that, so the words around it help you see that it is a list ending.
explain: Words can also grow from mixing cultures. Jazz and blues grew among African American communities, mixing African rhythms with European instruments. When you learn where a word comes from, its meaning often makes more sense.
fact: Jazz is a kind of music that began with African American musicians in New Orleans, USA.
fact: Jazz music has strong rhythms, brass instruments and lots of improvisation, which means making up music on the spot.
fact: To jazz something up means to make it brighter, livelier or more exciting.
fact: «And all that jazz» means and things like that, and it is informal American speech.
fact: Jazz grew among African American communities, mixing African rhythms with European instruments.
exercise: «Jazz players make up solos on the spot after years of practice.» What is jazz in this sentence? | A famous bridge | A hot drink | *A kind of music | A type of dance shoe
exercise: Mia wants to jazz up her bedroom. What does jazz up mean? | Pack everything into a box | Clean it under the bed | *Make it brighter and more exciting | Paint it grey and wet
exercise: Where did jazz begin? | Haiti | *New Orleans, USA | Scotland | Brazil
test: Which phrase means and things like that? | Jazz hands | *And all that jazz | Jazz band | Jazz up
test: Jazz grew from mixing what? | Sea shells and church bells | Only English words | Rain and sand | *African rhythms and European instruments
exam: In «Let's jazz up the party with some music», what does jazz up mean? | Count the guests | *Make it livelier and more exciting | Sleep quietly | Put it away
